# LINE ENDINGS — everything this repo ships is LF, on every platform.
#
# This plugin is CONSUMED as a checkout: `/plugin install` clones the
# marketplace, and the consumer then executes the bundled `hooks/**.sh` and
# `scripts/**.sh` directly. On a Windows host `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git
# for Windows default, so without this file every bundled script checks out
# with CRLF — and a CRLF shell script does not merely warn, it fails to PARSE
# (`syntax error near unexpected token $'{\r'`). Every hook lib and scan script
# dies, and `template-reconcile.sh` silently reports every anchor in a bundled
# Markdown template as a missing gap, because the CR-suffixed anchors can never
# match the consumer's LF file. See CHANGELOG "steer" and issue #438.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the index AND on checkout, regardless
# of the consumer's core.autocrlf. The explicit extension lines below are
# belt-and-braces for the file types whose CRLF failure mode is silent or
# fatal; `text=auto` alone would already cover them.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Bundled templates with no matching extension rule above (dotfiles are stored
# without their leading dot). They are shell/ini/Markdown-ish text and must land
# as LF too. Belt-and-braces: `* text=auto eol=lf` already covers them.
plugins/steer/templates/scaffold/gitignore                    text eol=lf
plugins/steer/templates/scaffold/gitattributes                text eol=lf
plugins/steer/templates/scaffold/env.example                  text eol=lf
plugins/steer/templates/scaffold/worktreeinclude              text eol=lf
plugins/steer/templates/scaffold/profiles/workspace/gitignore text eol=lf
plugins/steer/templates/docker/dockerignore                   text eol=lf

# Binaries must never be newline-normalized.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# CHANGELOG.md is GENERATED — `changie merge` assembles it from `.changes/`.
# Never edit it by hand (check_changelog.py fails when it drifts from the version
# files). No merge driver is needed: pending entries are one file per change
# under `.changes/unreleased/`, so concurrent PRs write different paths and can
# never collide. That is what retired the old `CHANGELOG.md merge=union` line —
# union is line-based, and the multi-line entries this repo writes are exactly
# the shape it splices together wrongly (the same reason spec/history/ is a
# directory; see plugins/steer/templates/spec/history-readme.md).
